---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [{
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: 'Where does BundleEmbedder get runtime.wasm?',
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: 'From your app bundle. BundleEmbedder in traverse-embedder-web loads runtime/runtime.wasm plus runtime/runtime.wasm.sha256 from the bundle, verifies the digest, and fails closed at init if the file is missing. The npm package does not ship runtime.wasm, and as of v0.16.1 no GitHub release ships it either: the last runtime-wasm-v* release is runtime-wasm-v0.11.0. Without building it yourself, fetch it from the release tag (for v0.16.1, runtime/runtime.wasm at tag v0.16.1, sha256 7a322a17e3fd58f8bd26e915dd81bad68732500dc981499bd280a6d2e6507d52) and check it against runtime/runtime-release.json at the same tag. How apps should get it is an open decision in traverse#1784.',
    },
  }],
});

const relatedLinks = [
  { href: '/questions/what-is-one-shared-runtime-wasm.html', label: 'What is one shared runtime.wasm in Traverse?' },
  { href: '/questions/does-the-rust-embedder-use-the-same-runtime-wasm.html', label: 'Does the Rust embedder use the same runtime.wasm?' },
  { href: '/questions/does-catalog-model-execute-run-on-published-traverse-packages.html', label: 'Does catalog model.execute run on published Traverse packages?' },
  { href: '/questions/how-do-i-upgrade-to-traverse-v0-16-0.html', label: 'How do I upgrade to Traverse v0.16.x?' },
];
---
<QuestionLayout
  title="Where does BundleEmbedder get runtime.wasm?"
  description="From your app bundle, digest-checked. npm traverse-embedder-web doesn't ship it, and no release has shipped it since runtime-wasm-v0.11.0, so today you take it from the release tag. Tracked in traverse#1784."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/where-does-bundleembedder-get-runtime-wasm.html"
  category="Runtime and architecture"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> from your app bundle. <code>BundleEmbedder</code> loads <code>runtime/runtime.wasm</code> and <code>runtime/runtime.wasm.sha256</code> from the bundle you give it, checks the digest, and fails closed at <code>init</code> if the file isn't there. The catch is where you get the file in the first place: today it comes from the git tag, not from a package or a release.</p>

  <h2>What ships where (v0.16.1)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>npm <code>traverse-embedder-web</code></strong> doesn't include <code>runtime.wasm</code>. Its README says so: the orchestrator belongs to the app bundle.</li>
    <li><strong>The v0.16.1 GitHub Release</strong> is notes only.</li>
    <li><strong>The last <code>runtime-wasm-v*</code> release</strong> is <code>runtime-wasm-v0.11.0</code> (September 16, 2026). That's far too old for a v0.16.x embedder.</li>
    <li><strong>The tag has it.</strong> <code>runtime/runtime.wasm</code> and <code>runtime/runtime-release.json</code> are committed at each release tag.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to do today</h2>
  <p>Take <code>runtime/runtime.wasm</code> from the tag that matches your <code>traverse-embedder-web</code> version, and check it against <code>runtime/runtime-release.json</code> from the same tag before you put it in your bundle. For v0.16.1:</p>
  <pre><code>curl -fsSLO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/traverse-framework/traverse/v0.16.1/runtime/runtime.wasm
sha256sum runtime.wasm
# expect 7a322a17e3fd58f8bd26e915dd81bad68732500dc981499bd280a6d2e6507d52</code></pre>
  <p><code>runtime-release.json</code> at v0.16.1 reads <code>runtime_version</code> 0.16.1, <code>bridge_version</code> 1.1.0, <code>bridge_abi_version</code> 10100, and the same sha256. Then write <code>sha256:&lt;hex&gt;</code> into <code>runtime/runtime.wasm.sha256</code> in your bundle. The published-package proof for <a href="/questions/does-catalog-model-execute-run-on-published-traverse-packages.html">catalog <code>model.execute</code></a> does exactly this, and its clean-runner CI checks the digest every run.</p>
  <p>Or build it yourself from the tagged source with Rust 1.95 or later. Either way you end up with one shared <code>runtime.wasm</code> that every web and Node host loads; native Rust hosts link <code>traverse-runtime</code> directly and don't need the file.</p>

  <h2>What's still open</h2>
  <p><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/issues/1784">traverse#1784</a> asks for a decision: publish a <code>runtime-wasm-vX.Y.Z</code> release with every product release (the release process still lists that type, but no workflow creates it), ship the file in the npm package, or document the tag as the supported source. Until that lands, treat the tag as the source and always check the digest.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
